  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 341 AM CDT Sun Sep 6 2026\n\nToday through tonight: Areas of fog are focused over the lower \nPine Belt region early this morning, and this will persist to \naround sunrise. Otherwise, expect another day of dangerous heat\nand have maintained the heat advisory that is in effect for\nthe entire area. Quick amplification of the upper level trough \nover the eastern CONUS will sent a front toward our forecast area.\nThere will be some added compressional heating ahead of the \nboundary with H850 temperatures in the low 20s deg C, and this \nwill result in continued elevated dangerous heat levels. A marginal\nsevere risk has emerged for this afternoon/evening as scattered \nstorms should initiate ahead of the front in an airmass \ncharacterized by steep low to mid level lapse rates. This will \nsupport severe wind gusts in the more intense storms. If storm \ndevelopment becomes more imminent, we could certainly see the risk\nlevel increase to slight (2 of 5) as there is recent precedence \nfor storms to overachieve with the hotter days and anomalously hot\nH850 temps. \n\nMonday through Saturday: A stubborn subtropical ridge will remain\nplanted over the Gulf Coast states, so don't expect signs for \nearly fall weather to show up any time soon. Instead, we can \nexpect additional limited to elevated dangerous heat threats and \nreduced rainfall coverage mostly limited to southern portions of \nthe area. /EC/",
